{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3099","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3099","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3099\",\"parent_revision\":\"10b18d52781c457af84a0a97869ba081f337da2356bd8e3cfee5d2e75fbe999c\",\"root_revision\":\"10b18d52781c457af84a0a97869ba081f337da2356bd8e3cfee5d2e75fbe999c\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: Ideas\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis is an exploration/experiment item. The deliverable is an experiment result or design decision with evidence (a prototype on a real plan, a measured comparison, or a written design verdict) \u2014 not a mandate to ship the feature. Origin: comparison of graph-engineering research against the development-harness architecture, grounded against the #3060 agent over-orchestration incident (a task-worker recursively dispatched sub-teams after receiving orchestrator-voiced prose; ~$100 of unplanned spend). Shared design constraint across all seven exploration items in this set: the model keeps the judgment (scoring, declaring, deciding semantic dependencies) but writes it down ONCE, as typed data, at planning time; the mechanical consequence is applied deterministically by machinery that already exists (sam_schema models, DependencyGraph, the implement-feature dispatch loop, work-milestone). No new scheduler, no generic graph runtime \u2014 that larger direction is explicitly out of scope for these items.\\n\\nWhat/why: Failure handling today is prose (\\\"after 3 cycles report BLOCKED\\\") plus one hardcoded behavior (DependencyGraph.mark_downstream_skipped). There is no defined policy at all for executor loss \u2014 the executor's environment disappearing mid-task (worktree vanished, session died). In the #3060 incident, a dispatched agent lost its worktree, had no defined recovery move, and improvised one: it wrote a delegation prompt handing orchestrator-voiced procedure (dh:implement-feature's Progress Loop) to a task-worker, which triggered recursive team/worktree creation and the bulk of the ~$100 unplanned spend. The failure was not the worktree loss; it was that recovery was left to improvisation.\\n\\nMitigates: improvised recovery after node failure (the direct #3060 cascade mechanism); inconsistent retry counting; silent divergence between what prose says should happen on failure and what agents actually do.\\n\\nBenefit lands in: the dispatch loop (reads the policy mechanically), incident containment (failures terminate at escalation instead of compounding), and cost control.\\n\\nProposed exploration: (1) add an on_failure field to the SAM task model with a small closed enum \u2014 retry(n), skip_downstream, escalate; (2) define executor-loss as its own event whose policy is always escalate \u2014 the worker's only legal move on losing its environment is to report up to its dispatcher, never to rebuild topology; (3) teach the dispatch loop to apply the policy; (4) validate by tabletop-replaying the #3060 incident sequence against the policy: confirm every decision point where improvisation occurred would instead have terminated in an escalation.\\n\\nRelated: Item 4 (\\\"Explore per-task executor-type declaration so dispatch capability is planned, never inferred\\\") \u2014 both are direct structural mitigations of the #3060 incident class. Item 6 (\\\"Explore attempts/max_attempts counters on SAM tasks replacing prose retry rules\\\") \u2014 retry counting becomes data.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Experiment or design exploration executed as described (prototype on a real plan, measured comparison, or written design verdict)\\n- [ ] Decision recorded with evidence: adopt, adapt, or reject \u2014 with rationale\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **explore declared per-task failure policies (retry/skip/escalate) including executor-loss** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: graph-engineering research comparison\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ba8d2e91dccfbe1632f6dfc04f9afd4181adf4a7aedfc3db046aca9100b36f7e\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIpH8Q\"}"}